  "discussions_data": [
    {
      "id": "5b6b49fa58b75ddb36d5e1ddfa19b602d2dff4d7",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1599594687,
          "type": null,
          "body": "You might want to call this `ws_ascii_strcasestr()`, as that's really what it is.\n\n(For fun, the glibc `strcasestr()`, with ASCII-for-English-style case-mapping, could be used, as that's apparently faster than the BSD one; I haven't looked at it to see what it does, e.g. [Boyer-Moore](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boyer–Moore_string-search_algorithm) or [Knuth-Morris-Pratt](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knuth–Morris–Pratt_algorithm) or something else.  Extra credit for picking up the fast assembler versions - I think those use various flavors of [SIMD-within-a-register](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SWAR).)",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2023-10-12T00:40:20.738Z",
          "updated_at": "2023-10-12T00:40:20.738Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 256356364,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 12552,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "43e12b46b601d001db3eeb748ac24c5ce77d83b4",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1599609807,
          "type": null,
          "body": "The GNULib `c-strcasestr()` and the glib ones are both LGPLv2.1; I would guess that they're somewhat similar. The former describes itself as:\n\n>  /* We use the Two-Way string matching algorithm (also known as Chrochemore-Perrin), which guarantees linear complexity with constant space.  Additionally, for long needles, we also use a bad character shift table similar to the Boyer-Moore algorithm to achieve improved (potentially sub-linear) performance. */\n\nWe could also get a more recent (but still LGPL v2.1) memmem; a decent memrchr implementation would be useful for Find Packet searching through Packet Bytes in reverse order allowing multiple matches per frame, which is how I started to look at this in the first place.)",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/99c30adacedf45514aaf711ba8a44f56322ebb53e9b550b7e3c9cd83bd6a1408?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/johnthacker"
          },
          "created_at": "2023-10-12T01:22:06.999Z",
          "updated_at": "2023-10-12T01:22:06.999Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 256356364,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
